A||N||13||Butter||Runnin' the Tires Off Rim... Helppp......||||1||
Z||000000||Butter||12-27-2001||01:24 PM||||Ok Folks,<BR>Its like the subject says.  I'm havin' trouble running the tires off the rim while cornering.  I have a v8ed 84 that weighs in at 2750 lbs and I'm running 215/60-14 tires.  I like to corner hard when I'm feelin' froggy and conditions permit and have had this happen twice this year.  <P>Went threw the neighbor's fence the first time with minimum damage to the fence and car.  I was going approximately 70mph when it occured then.  It was a Lee tire.<P>This time I was going approximately 30mph when it happened and I managed to keep it between the ditches and on my side of the road.  This time the inside sidewall blew almost totally out.  It was a Goodyear Eagle.<P>Both times this happened the air pressure was recently checked at 30-32psi and appeared properly inflated.<P>I've been wanting to go to a size 15" rim and maybe a shorter sidewall but didn't know if this could help the problem.  Can any of yall tire guru's or motorcrosser's help me.  These occurances has a tendancy to make one draw up so tight I couldn't fart if my stomach was full of dynomite.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||jelly2m8||12-27-2001||01:36 PM||||I'd suggest going with a low profile tire of a top brand.<P> Also you may want to go with a larger wheel, 16 or 17 inch.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-27-2001||02:35 PM||||lower profile tires will help. When you corner hard the sidewalls roll under and 'pop' You can also run higher air pressure. Auto crossing my Mercedes, a friend who races SCCA Mercedes told me to keep 45 pnds in tires if I was using street tires. You probably be best going with like 17" rim and 35/40 series, but your ride will suffer.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-27-2001||02:39 PM||||First, you need to get performance tires. I would recommend an Ultra-Performace tire in the "Z" range. Going to a larger wheel size with a lower profile would also be good. Don't use regular replacements for your car. They won't hold up.<P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Butter||12-27-2001||03:10 PM||||Thanks Yall,<BR>I will keep an eye out for some of all yall recomend and come up with a solution.  I didn't want to mess with the ride too much but I understand in order to have as much fun as I have had safely, the ride will have to suffer.<P>I had some other reasons that I wanted to try to go with a 15" rim.  Do yall have any tire recomendations that might work well with that size rim?<P>Oreif, your right!<BR>Happiness is the corner. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||12-27-2001||03:56 PM||||I am using a set of 87 GT wheels painted black (refinished myself and using Rodney's center cap decals) with a set of BFGoodrich 225/50R15 Euro T/A's. I have been around corners at excessive speeds including highway exit ramps at speeds faster than "normally allowed". My tires do not roll under in hard cornering and the ride of the car isn't too much different then when I had my 14" wheels/tires on it.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wheel.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Butter||12-27-2001||04:25 PM||||Oreif,<BR>Those look nice.  This is more like the side wall appearance I am looking for if the performance comes with it.  Is there a certain rating I need to look for as you mentioned earlier?  I know there is a certain wear rating on tires and I understand there is also a speed rating.  Do you know how to read these ratings or where I might find a site to read up on it at?<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-27-2001||06:26 PM||||any tire store or website will have the ratings,<P>I think S is up to 112 mph<P>H is up to 120<P>V/VR is up to 150<P>Z is over<P>dont remember for sure, they change that stuff once in a while, havent bought a set for a few years.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||12-27-2001||06:35 PM||||The Euro T/A's are H rated tires. You can get the entire spec listing at:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.bfgoodrichtires.com" TARGET=_blank>www.bfgoodrichtires.com</A> <P>They also have other tires that can go faster and have listings of all the different specs like speed, wear, traction, etc. I went with the Euro's because they keep the speedo the same. A 225/50R15 has the same outside diameter as a 215/60R14. The difference is it's wider and the sidewalls are lower/stronger.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.bfgoodrichtires.com/catalog/euro_ta.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bfgoodrichtires.com/catalog/euro_ta.html</A> <P>Here's all the speed ratings:<P><BR>Q-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 100 mph.* <P> <BR>S-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 112 mph.* <P> <BR>T-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 118 mph.*<P> <BR>U-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 124 mph.* <P> <BR>H-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 130 mph.* <P> <BR>V-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 149 mph.*<P> <BR>W-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 168 mph.* <P> <BR>Y-speed rated  Capable of speeds up to 186 mph.* <P> <BR>Z-speed rated  Capable of speeds above 149 mph.* <BR> <BR>*Exceeding the lawful speed limit is neither recommended nor endorsed and will make you really unpopular with the local constabulary. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||12-27-2001||06:54 PM||||You need 'ultra performance" Z-rated tyres.<P>Pirelli Pzeros, Contentintal ContiSports, etc.<P>You *NEED* bigger rims!!!<P>a V8 fiero is just plain unsafe on 14" rims with anything close to stock radius rubber.<P>I would recomend a good set of fordged multipiece 17" wheels (or 18 if you can afford it) and some good<P>Sizes to think of (Fieros really like):<P>17x7 front with 215/45/17<BR>17x8 rear with  245/40/17 (possibly 235/45/17 if the 245s rub)<P>Get Pirelli Pzero Asymetrico front and rear.<P>Enjoy mashing it in the twisties with confidence!<P>Here's a tip: the ride quality actually goes UP in my opinion.  It's a little more 'intamite' with the road, but *nowhere* nearly as bad as I had espected!  The ride at speed (60+mph, especially above 90) acutally got *BETTER*.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||12-27-2001||08:03 PM||||Note: my v8 car is reciving some pretty crazy tyres:<P>18x.7.5 front with 225/40/18<BR>18x10   rear  with 265/35/18<P>They're very huge, but actually a tad lighter than the chunky 15" alloys the Fiero came from the factory with. Even in the rear...<P>I will report on how they ride (and turn  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||Kasdan||12-27-2001||08:51 PM||||Ive got 205/60/15 Dayton Daytona HR's on all 4 corners, stock 15" wheels.  Anyone know what these tires are rated?<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000012||jelly2m8||12-27-2001||10:29 PM|||| Orief, I'm running the same brand/model size as you.<P> So far I am really amazed with these tires.<P>They are a great alternative for the mid-line tires. I think another set of these will be going on when these ones wear out.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ronnie||12-27-2001||10:30 PM||||Don't most tires have their rating on the sidewall? It should be listed in all that writing on the tire.||||reg||6
